@brief
Ctrl+F in VS Code opens a little box. In vim, search IS a motion:
/thing jumps there, and because it is a motion you can also say
d/thing to delete everything up to it.

The one you will use most is  *  : put the cursor on any word and
press *, and vim searches for that exact word. No typing, no box.

Read order.repository.ts and fill in answers.txt.

Your vimrc already sets hlsearch, so matches stay highlighted.
Clear the highlight with Ctrl-L when it gets noisy.

@keys
/text Enter    search forwards        ?text Enter   search backwards
n  N           next match / previous match
*  #           search for the word under the cursor, fwd / back
Ctrl-L         clear the search highlight (set up in your vimrc)
:%s/x//gn      count matches of x without changing anything
:noh           same as Ctrl-L, clears the highlight
d/text Enter   delete from here up to the next "text"
/text\c        search ignoring case for this one search

@hints
:%s/customerId//gn prints "N matches on N lines" at the bottom and
changes nothing. The gn flag is the trick - g means every match on
each line, n means "just count".
--
Beware: /records also matches "record" inside other words? No - but
it does match "records" inside nothing else here. Whole-word search
is  /\<records\>  , and * does that for you automatically.
--
For line numbers, turn on :set number , then /toEntity and read the
line you land on. Press n to walk to the later matches.
--
?deleted searches BACKWARDS from the cursor. Press G first to get to
the bottom, then ?deleted finds the last one directly.

@solution
:set number
:%s/customerId//gn     -> the count
:%s/records//gn        -> the count
gg /toEntity Enter     -> first match, read the line number
G  ?deleted Enter      -> last match, read the line number

:bn  fill in answers.txt  :wa

The habit: * is free. Cursor on a symbol, press *, you are at the
next use of it. It replaces "select the word, Ctrl+F, Enter".

@start answers.txt
